---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.1 — while Loops &amp; The WhileLab Algorithms Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.1, 2.7 · 90-Minute Unified Build.</strong> A <code>while</code> loop
    repeats a block of code as long as a boolean condition remains <code>true</code> — making it
    the right tool whenever the number of iterations is <em>unknown in advance</em>. On the AP CS A
    exam, <code>while</code> loops appear in two classic forms: state-driven simulations (where a
    quantity like HP or energy ticks up and down until a threshold is crossed) and integer digit
    extraction (using <code>% 10</code> and <code>/ 10</code>).
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Starting Files (Download Both):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/WhileLab.java" download>WhileLab.java</a> — bare-minimum compiling stub.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/WhileLabCheck.java" download>WhileLabCheck.java</a> — automated AP-style
            test suite (19 checks across 4 parts).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>javac WhileLab.java WhileLabCheck.java
java WhileLabCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>roundsToSurvivePoison(int startingHP, int poisonPerRound, int regenPerRound)</code></h3>

    <p>
        A hero enters a toxic swamp with <code>startingHP</code> hit points. During each round:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>First, poison strikes:</strong> subtract <code>poisonPerRound</code> from the
            hero's HP.</li>
        <li><strong>Second, check for survival:</strong> if the hero's HP is still strictly greater
            than <code>0</code>, their amulet restores <code>regenPerRound</code> HP. If their HP
            dropped to <code>0</code> or below from the poison tick, the hero dies immediately and
            regeneration does <strong>not</strong> occur.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        Your method returns the total number of rounds the hero was subjected to poison before
        dying (or <code>0</code> if <code>startingHP &lt;= 0</code> to begin with). If the hero
        will survive <strong>forever</strong> (an infinite loop!), your method must return
        <code>-1</code> without freezing.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 1: The Round-1 Lethal Tick vs. Infinite Regen</h3>
        <p>
            To avoid an infinite loop, most programmers immediately write this guard at the top of
            <code>roundsToSurvivePoison</code>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Naive infinite-loop guard — why does this fail Check 4 in WhileLabCheck?
if (regenPerRound &gt;= poisonPerRound) {
    return -1;
}</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>Trace <code>roundsToSurvivePoison(5, 5, 10)</code>: the hero starts with
                <code>5 HP</code>, takes <code>5</code> poison per round, and regenerates
                <code>10</code> HP per round. Even though <code>regenPerRound &gt;= poisonPerRound</code>,
                what happens on Round 1 when poison hits <em>before</em> regeneration? Why does the
                naive guard above return <code>-1</code> when the correct answer is <code>1</code>?</li>
            <li>What is the exact condition under which a hero with <code>startingHP &gt; 0</code>
                truly survives forever? (Check both <code>poisonPerRound &lt;= 0</code> and what
                must be true after Round 1's poison tick!)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Parts 2 &amp; 3 · AP Digit Extraction</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Parts 2 &amp; 3: <code>sumRuneDigits(int runeCode)</code> &amp; <code>countEvenDigits(int runeCode)</code></h3>

    <p>
        One of the most frequent algorithms on the AP CS A exam peels digits off a non-negative
        integer from right to left using two arithmetic operators:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>runeCode % 10</code> — extracts the <strong>rightmost digit</strong>
            (e.g., <code>4729 % 10</code> is <code>9</code>).</li>
        <li><code>runeCode / 10</code> — removes the rightmost digit via integer division
            (e.g., <code>4729 / 10</code> is <code>472</code>).</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 2: The Premature Return &amp; The Zero Edge Case</h3>
        <p>
            Look at two common bugs when students write <code>countEvenDigits(int runeCode)</code>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Bug A: Premature else-return inside the loop
while (runeCode &gt; 0) {
    int digit = runeCode % 10;
    if (digit % 2 == 0) {
        count++;
    } else {
        return count;   // &lt;-- What does this do on 20405?!
    }
    runeCode /= 10;
}

// Bug B: What happens when runeCode starts at 0?
// How many times does while (runeCode &gt; 0) run when runeCode == 0?</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>In Bug A, why does putting <code>return count;</code> inside the loop stop after the
                very first odd digit (<code>5</code> in <code>20405</code>), never inspecting <code>0</code>, <code>4</code>, <code>0</code>, or <code>2</code>?</li>
            <li>The integer <code>0</code> has one digit, and <code>0</code> is an even number (<code>0 % 2 == 0</code>),
                so <code>countEvenDigits(0)</code> must return <code>1</code>. Why does a plain
                <code>while (runeCode &gt; 0)</code> loop return <code>0</code> for
                <code>countEvenDigits(0)</code>, and how do you guard for it?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h4>AP Specification — Parts 2 &amp; 3</h4>
    <ul>
        <li><code>public static int sumRuneDigits(int runeCode)</code> — precondition:
            <code>runeCode &gt;= 0</code>. Returns the sum of all base-10 digits in
            <code>runeCode</code>.</li>
        <li><code>public static int countEvenDigits(int runeCode)</code> — precondition:
            <code>runeCode &gt;= 0</code>. Returns the count of even base-10 digits (<code>0, 2, 4, 6, 8</code>)
            in <code>runeCode</code>. Note that <code>countEvenDigits(0)</code> returns <code>1</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · AP FRQ Synthesis Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>manaCrystalSteps(int startingEnergy)</code></h3>

    <p>
        An unstable mana crystal oscillates according to the Collatz (Hailstone) rules until its
        energy stabilizes at <code>1</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>If <code>startingEnergy &lt;= 1</code>, it is already stable; return <code>0</code> steps.</li>
        <li>While energy is greater than <code>1</code>:
            <ul>
                <li>If energy is <strong>even</strong>, divide it by <code>2</code>.</li>
                <li>If energy is <strong>odd</strong>, multiply it by <code>3</code> and add <code>1</code>.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>Count and return the total number of transformations required to reach <code>1</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint — <code>if / else</code> vs. Two Separate <code>if</code>s Inside a Loop</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Suppose a student writes two separate <code>if</code> statements instead of
                <code>if / else</code> inside the <code>while</code> loop:
                <pre><code>if (energy % 2 != 0) {
    energy = energy * 3 + 1;
}
if (energy % 2 == 0) {
    energy = energy / 2;
}</code></pre>
                Trace what happens on a single iteration when <code>energy</code> starts at
                <code>3</code>. Why does the second <code>if</code> immediately fire in the same
                iteration, and how does <code>else</code> prevent that?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><code>java WhileLabCheck</code> reports <strong><code>19 passed, 0 failed</code></strong></li>
            <li>You have completed the reflection boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/while-loops.html">while Loops</a> — initialization, test, update, infinite loop guards</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/arithmetic.html">Arithmetic &amp; Modulo</a> — % 10 and / 10 digit extraction</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
